soc: sunxi: h713-arisc: the EDID version byte is a per-port block mask

SetEDIDVersion does not take a version. Its byte is a bitmask over the
firmware's three HDMI ports: the publish routine walks the port map records
and gives a port the second of the two 256-byte blocks in the store when that
port's bit is set in it, and the first block otherwise (0x11df0..0x11e28).
The handler masks the argument to four bits, keeps it at g_Data + 4 and
republishes only the ports whose bit changed; ResetEDIDModule clears it, so a
reset puts every port back on the first block (0x12188). This driver has been
sending the constant 2 -- bit 1, port 1, a port with no socket on any board we
have -- and calling it "version 2", which left "what do 0 and 1 mean" on the
open list for two weeks while the answer was in the dispatcher (analysis
AP3l section 1, confirming AP3h section 5).

So the byte gets the name it has. arisc_hdmi_edid_mask() is the kernel API,
one bit per port, values above 0x0f refused rather than silently truncated
the way the firmware would truncate them, and the value in force is board
policy on the arisc node: allwinner,edid-version-mask, optional, default 0.

0 is not merely the safe value, it is the right one on both boards, and the
argument is the display firmware's mode table rather than the panel. The two
halves of the EDID file are identical up to byte 129 and carry two different
CEA extensions from byte 130 on. The second adds 3840x2160p50/p60 and
4096x2160p50/p60 in 4:2:0, 1080p100/120 and a Dolby Vision block, and lowers
the HDMI vendor block's maximum TMDS clock from 340 MHz to 300 MHz. Those
four 4K modes are exactly the rows the display firmware's own table has
marked disabled (T1 section 5), so serving the second block invites every
source into modes this receiver is structurally unable to lock while lowering
the ceiling for the ones it can. The first block stops where that table
stops, at 4K30 / 297 MHz, which the HY310 locked on 21.09. (doku/128 §2).
Both panels scale, so panel resolution does not enter into it on either
machine.

Nothing changes for a source on port 0. Bit 0 of the constant 2 was clear, so
port 0 has been served the first block since the first successful sequence,
and the default mask 0 serves it the same block. What changes is port 1,
which loses a second block nothing was reading, and the driver's own story
about why.

The command also moves behind the eight fragments. Sent before the upload it
publishes on every port whose bit moves -- and what it publishes then is the
firmware's own ROM default, because the store still holds it. With the old
constant that was port 1 and nobody saw it; with a mask containing port 0 it
would be a stale EDID at the source until ours followed. Behind fragment 7 it
is idempotent by construction: fragment 7 has already published all three
ports with the mask in force, so the command republishes only what changed,
or returns at 0x125f0 having done nothing. CheckEDIDUpdateStatus stays last
and remains the proof that fragment 7 arrived. This is not "as the vendor
does it": nothing in tvserver or libvideo ever calls the EDID path at all
(AP3h section 4), so there is no stock order to copy.

Two readbacks come with it, because the one this driver had could not tell
the two blocks apart: it asked for 64 bytes and logged 16, and the blocks
agree for their first 130. The bring-up now reads all 256 and logs the 16
bytes from offset 128, where byte 130 alone separates them (0x4c against
0x54), and debugfs gains edid-port0 with the whole block as the DDC RAM
holds it. The debugfs status file gains the mask the firmware holds next to
the one the board asked for -- they part company if anything else resets the
EDID module -- and cmd gains "edid-version <mask>" so the choice can be made
on a bench without a rebuild.

Two corrections ride along, both comments. The port map record's byte +1 is
not an unused tag: the publish routine copies it into the published EDID at
the HDMI vendor block's physical-address byte and then recomputes the
extension checksum, which is where the two-byte difference between the file
and what a source reads comes from. And HPD_DOWN_MS loses its second
justification: the stock HDMI_SetHPDTimeInterval(0xc8) this file cited goes
through THal_Vp_HDMI_SetHPDTimeInterval, a cpu_comm call into the MIPS
receiver, and never reaches this core (AP3h section 6, R3). The value is
unchanged; the device measurement in doku/nachtlog/A2-hpd-dauer-edid.md was
always the real source.

Source: umbau/re-apps/AP3l/ (REVIEW.md and design A), AP3h sections 4-6.
The HY310 sees: port 0 keeps block 0, byte for byte. Only port 1's block and
the driver's log lines change.

